  "query_preview": "..Mostly dry conditions are expected across the forecast \nthrough the next week. \n\nThrough tonight...weak flow remains across the region through \ntonight keeping us cool and dry with light winds.  \n\nFriday...a weak but fast moving cut-off low will swing through the \nregion on Friday. While we have been able to dry out over the past \nfew days, there is still enough moisture around for some isolatd to \nscattered showers to develop Friday afternoon. With cooler air \naloft, there will be enough instability to allow for some \nthunderstorms to develop. Best chances for any precipitation on \nFriday will be over eastern Arizona and across the higher terrain. \nRainfall amounts are expected to remain light and generally around \n0.10\" or less with a few locally higher amounts in heavier showers. \nAs the low pulls away from the region, colder air will be drawn down \nto the surface and some snow may begin to mix in. Best chances to \nsee snow look to be in areas above 8000', like Buffalo Pass in the \nChuskas, with little to no accumulation expected.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...The departing low sinks to the south through \nthe weekend. The pressure gradient does tighten up a bit so expect \nsome breezy conditions. Winds will be out of the north to northeast \naround 5-15 mph in most areas with gusts to 20-35 mph along and \nsouth/southwest of the higher terrain. \n\nMonday and onward...The pattern through the extended part of the \nforecast looks to remain mostly dry. A weak trough may cross through \nsouthern Arizona on Tuesday, but with very limited moisture it is \nunlikely we will see much more than an isolated shower or two \ndevelop, mainly towards the White Mountains. Expect mild \ntemperatures and dry conditions to continue through much of the \ncoming week. Looking further out and towards the end of next week, \nthe models show a deepening west coast trough that becomes a cut-off \nlow. Current tracks keep the low off to our south which would limit \nand moisture returns into the region. However, a shift northward \ncould bring another round of showers to our area. Overall, expect a \nrelatively uneventful weather week with the potential for a chance \nlater next week.",
